#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218195} :activity/id #uuid "0db9329c-dec7-5a3e-a676-325cf54ff03f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050895 :topic/id #uuid "6f396a30-c21c-5a35-935d-80293083bda2" :topic/math-academy-id 6367} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218195 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218358 :tutorial/content "The Wronskian is a quick way to check whether two solutions of a homogeneous ODE are linearly independent.\n\nIn this lesson, we learn an identity, which tells us how the Wronskian changes with $x$ for an equation of the form \n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the second-order linear homogeneous ODE above on some interval $I.$ Let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian:\n![](images/bf/bf982a342681e4142c476f52c77cb95836bc024d75a0c62961fc5f0d6d71320b.svg)\n\nThen, **Abel's identity** states that the Wronskian satisfies the first-order linear differential equation\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nSolving this equation via separation of variables gives the general form for $W(x){:}$\n\n$$\nW(x) = C e^{-\\int P(x) \\, \\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nThis allows us to determine the behavior of the Wronskian without knowing the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2.$\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''+3xy'+e^x y=0\n$$\n\nwith solutions $y_1$ and $y_2,$ and the corresponding Wronskian $W(x).$ Let's determine the differential equation that $W(x)$ must satisfy.\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =-3xW(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If the equation is not already in standard form, we should rewrite it before applying Abel’s identity." :tutorial/id #uuid "df1133ef-ff8b-5e3c-bbce-c0f1a484db10" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14746 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e7527729-b857-5e9b-bea8-a7cbd99e635d" :step/math-academy-id 600667 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218196}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218196 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217244 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217003 :question/id #uuid "f6884f33-97b6-502e-91c8-bdd3a4fc95d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21868"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050150}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497403}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation"} :step/id #uuid "bb9b873b-720d-52a3-af19-21b0736163e7" :step/math-academy-id 600668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218197}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218197 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217245 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217004 :question/id #uuid "bac67ae0-dc3c-528c-9e07-7a81caa86db4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21869"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050905}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552436}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"} :step/id #uuid "21049594-a386-58b7-948b-933fb27c4926" :step/math-academy-id 600671 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218200}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218198 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217246 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217005 :question/id #uuid "008cfc63-46d4-5e99-84a2-76df45a51001" :question/math-academy-id "e-21870"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497407}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"} :step/id #uuid "1a5dec47-1b73-5c78-99d1-b02f62ec0319" :step/math-academy-id 600674 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218199}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218199 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218359 :tutorial/content "*Abel's identity* says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their *Wronskian* $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-p(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nTo prove it, we start with the definition\n\n$$\nW(x)=y_1y_2'-y_2y_1'\n$$\n\nand differentiate both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}}+{y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-\\cancel{{y}_{2}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of $y''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny_1''=-p(x)y_1'-q(x)y_1\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny_2''=-p(x)y_2'-q(x)y_2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into the expression for $W'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(-p(x){y}_{2}^{′}-q(x){y}_{2})-{y}_{2}(-p(x){y}_{1}^{′}-q(x){y}_{1}) \\\\  & =-p(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}}+p(x){y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}+\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}} \\\\  & =-p(x)({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}) \\\\  & =-p(x)W(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $W'(x)=-p(x)W(x)$, as required." :tutorial/id #uuid "e1263afb-6125-5c5f-8c88-5025fd9457db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14753 :tutorial/title "The Proof of Abel's Identity"} :step/id #uuid "888a5fbb-9e7e-52b5-8de1-71d906dc8972" :step/math-academy-id 600678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218200 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218360 :tutorial/content "Because the exponential factor in Abel's identity is *never zero*, we can deduce the following facts:\n\n- If $W(x_0)=0$ at some point $x_0 \\in I,$ then $W(x) = 0$ for all $x \\in I.$\n\n- If $W(x_0)\\neq 0,$ then $W(x) \\neq 0$ for all $x \\in I.$\n\nLet's see how this works on concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "1ff5ce47-4514-52a4-9f42-103d6b73b928" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14754 :tutorial/title "Applications of Abel's Identity"} :step/id #uuid "f86b886d-8ad5-5177-85d6-59b75ecbb221" :step/math-academy-id 600679 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218198}}] :activity/title "Abel's Identity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186217326}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552607} :activity/id #uuid "6acdc283-cb52-51b2-859b-9a24873f69e1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050895 :topic/id #uuid "6f396a30-c21c-5a35-935d-80293083bda2" :topic/math-academy-id 6367} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552528 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c115289-a055-5b27-80d7-61934239ac5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{e}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551694}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551694} :answer-field/id #uuid "beb68067-4a29-5062-a362-e53691a64280" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4b516dd7-0e0d-5e63-914a-7e40f288e42a" :question/math-academy-id "q-343270" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(-1)=6.$ What is $W(2)?$\n\n$W(2)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(-1)=6$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(-1) & =C{e}^{-(-1)} \\\\ 6 & =C{e}^{1} \\\\ C & =6{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=6{e}^{-1}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =6{e}^{-1}{e}^{-2}=6{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "010efac7-1b1a-535f-9cb9-4b100c90519f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552553}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6c623a5-d649-5a54-8c2b-52ae41579420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552065}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552065} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5aac8d3-3126-59bd-b7a3-08af8b0916d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0445e2f8-6c9c-5e30-8a27-7e22ee9c426c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343282" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+1}{y}^{′}+({x}^{2}+1)y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=6.$ What is $W(3)?$\n\n$W(3)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{x+1}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{x+1}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}(x+1)^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=6$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{1+1} \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ C & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{12}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =\\frac{12}{3+1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2bd25181-6ec4-5dc5-a8d2-76567a249e0e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c67e515c-772b-57db-ad7e-bc3bafd270b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316240}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316240} :answer-field/id #uuid "e40c8ae7-36d2-5187-91d9-5e9873b26864" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c8b7de5b-aac2-510e-98ee-6d02baa0c52b" :question/math-academy-id "q-343279" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+(\\frac{2}{x}+1){y}^{′}+(2-x)y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=3.$ What is $W(2)?$\n\n$W(2)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2}{x}+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -(\\frac{2}{x}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=3$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{{1}^{2}}{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3 & =C{e}^{-1} \\\\ C & =3e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{3e}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{3e}{{2}^{2}}{e}^{-2}=\\frac{3}{4e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3b195e0b-a3b0-54f2-986d-a168cb248436" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552548}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397617 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "304d5117-ee46-5c5d-afa7-ca920f029e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397619} {:answer/id #uuid "ae08826c-6c1c-5987-ad7e-864c4360f574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397620} {:answer/id #uuid "b83dbcc8-f9de-571f-b680-6fc8b19ba9d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397619} :answer-field/id #uuid "622cd65e-d7fb-587b-8c10-1defb970706b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397618} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ef66bf4-a759-5faa-bbe6-c8479e7aca1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4Q(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397623} {:answer/id #uuid "07f88880-7512-5cb1-85f0-9acaa96748b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397624} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd7fc4f-0bb8-5d15-9606-f2346f18bdfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397625} {:answer/id #uuid "52543647-4678-5589-9673-644a893cea31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4Q(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397626}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397625} :answer-field/id #uuid "80ef2068-6b95-504c-8d87-420eff7181f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397622} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e8310ce-fbd3-5538-b766-45d5514898c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397628} {:answer/id #uuid "e082f656-48a6-5575-b482-8353f60d9a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397629} {:answer/id #uuid "1d0a044d-7c2c-5bb4-bfbf-18edf83e4c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397630} {:answer/id #uuid "b96d58ae-2f44-580e-81fa-71567a86ef0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397631}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397631} :answer-field/id #uuid "70866db8-d47c-5d65-8fab-fa76cb30f2a9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "65f6d9f7-7688-5460-b686-2b9904fe578a" :question/math-academy-id "q-343289" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=-1.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=-1,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=4W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =4\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=-1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{4\\cdot 0} \\\\ -1 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=-{e}^{4x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"} :step/id #uuid "918f5bcc-15f1-5abf-893b-3349543fd788"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d3960c7-46d4-54fb-9a80-a9bd29e566d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2q(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315382} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ae41dc-ad99-5cda-ba2d-713a7eba96f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316154} {:answer/id #uuid "cc060b49-1a48-569b-85f6-6daa58b3c662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316274} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f654d4-27f6-5df4-ab8a-9b1d24769e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2q(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316274} :answer-field/id #uuid "0604709d-9626-5dae-9048-ebe10f78f09d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316563} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c13f347-3b30-5294-b6f9-46d6995cf777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315187} {:answer/id #uuid "146f7543-b9e5-51dc-a9d8-ae7096ca9c59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315241} {:answer/id #uuid "360ca319-c367-5456-95fc-0950b0f34989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315434} {:answer/id #uuid "d96dcda9-8117-5826-8af2-5a84d7d6521f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316329} :answer-field/id #uuid "58339bb5-e525-5e23-8c9a-df4ecfa1365d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316751} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ae3d01a-f8f9-5f0a-a61b-0b8fbeb38fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315176} {:answer/id #uuid "1c0f7d2e-035d-55bb-81e2-bdb00cba4ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315282} {:answer/id #uuid "281e05ce-1a19-5a40-a758-f08bd21f5d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315348}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315282} :answer-field/id #uuid "b15c37ad-1987-5287-8c8f-8494de399fe3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7ac49bd9-9719-507d-b361-709e747ff5e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-342724" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=1.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=1,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=2W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =2W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =2\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =2x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{2x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{2\\cdot 0} \\\\ 1 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)={e}^{2x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"} :step/id #uuid "d28590f4-cec9-5f5d-8729-6edf18e4059e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552528}}] :activity/title "Abel's Identity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551654}]}
